---
// src/pages/terminal.astro — /terminal: browser-based remote shell on your
// Rootz device. Connects through the relay with an E2E-encrypted session
// (apps/rootzd/docs/e2e-protocol.md); the relay never sees plaintext.
// Pure vanilla JS (no framework island): same pattern as account.astro.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
---

<Base
  title="Terminal"
  description="Open a Rootz Terminal on your device from the browser — end-to-end encrypted through the relay. Rootz Pro feature."
  banner
>
  <div class="container px-5 py-12">
    <h1 class="mt-0 mb-2">Terminal</h1>
    <p class="muted">
      A shell on your device, from this browser. The connection is
      end-to-end encrypted — the relay in between forwards only ciphertext.
      A <a href="/rootz">Rootz Pro</a> feature.
    </p>

    <div class="panel">
      <h2>Connect</h2>
      <p class="break-words text-body text-[1.15rem]">
        Enter your 16-digit account number — the devices paired to it load
        automatically. Pick one, then press Connect. A device appears here
        once the Rootz app on it has been paired (Account pairing →
        Pair &amp; start relay daemon). First phone? The four steps below
        take a minute.
      </p>
      <div class="stack">
        <input
          id="account-number"
          class="text-[1.2rem]"
          type="text"
          inputmode="numeric"
          autocomplete="off"
          spellcheck="false"
          placeholder="1234 5678 9012 3456"
        />
        <div class="row">
          <select id="device-select" class="min-w-0 flex-1 text-[1.2rem]" aria-label="Device">
            <option value="">Devices load once your account number is complete…</option>
          </select>
          <button type="button" class="btn" id="load-devices-btn">Refresh list</button>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" id="connect-btn">Connect</button>
          <button type="button" class="btn" id="disconnect-btn" disabled>Disconnect</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p id="conn-status" class="text-body text-[1.15rem]">Not connected.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <h2>First time? Set up the phone (once)</h2>
      <ol class="text-body text-[1.05rem]">
        <li class="mb-2">
          <strong class="text-ink">Turn on Wireless debugging.</strong>
          On the phone: Settings → System → Developer options →
          <em>Wireless debugging</em> → on, then accept “Always allow on this
          network”. The phone starts advertising its pairing and connect
          ports.
        </li>
        <li class="mb-2">
          <strong class="text-ink">Arm the pairing notification.</strong>
          Open <a href="/rootz">Rootz</a> → the <em>Wireless debugging</em>
          card → <strong>ARM PAIRING NOTIFICATION</strong>, and allow
          notifications when asked. This is what lets you type the code
          without leaving Settings — switching apps kills the pairing
          server.
        </li>
        <li class="mb-2">
          <strong class="text-ink">Pair the app.</strong>
          In Settings tap <em>Pair device with pairing code</em> — a 6-digit
          code appears. Pull down the notification shade, tap
          <strong>Pair</strong> on the Rootz notification, type the code and
          send. The Settings dialog closes itself once the phone accepts the
          key. You only do this once per phone.
        </li>
        <li class="mb-2">
          <strong class="text-ink">Wait for “ready”, then connect.</strong>
          The Rootz card shows
          <code class="text-ink">wireless adb: ready — ip:port</code> and the
          relay card shows <code class="text-ink">relay: up</code>. Enter
          your 16-digit account number above and the phone appears in the
          device list.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="text-body text-[1rem]">
        After a reboot the relay reconnects on its own — Rootz runs it as a
        foreground service with a “Rootz relay” notification, so the device
        stays reachable with the app closed. Android can switch Wireless
        debugging off during a reboot: if commands answer
        <code class="text-ink">wireless adb not ready</code>, open Rootz →
        <em>Open developer options</em> → <em>Wireless debugging</em> → turn it
        on, or off and on again if it was already on, then wait for the card
        to read <code class="text-ink">ready</code>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <h2>Device</h2>
      <dl class="row flex-wrap gap-x-8">
        <div>
          <dt class="text-body text-[1rem]">device_id (verified)</dt>
          <dd class="m-0 break-all" id="devinfo-id">—</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-body text-[1rem]">model</dt>
          <dd class="m-0" id="devinfo-name">—</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-body text-[1rem]">tier</dt>
          <dd class="m-0" id="devinfo-tier">—</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-body text-[1rem]">daemon</dt>
          <dd class="m-0" id="devinfo-version">—</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="panel">
      <h2>Shell</h2>
      <div id="terminal-log" class="termlog" aria-live="polite"></div>
      <div class="row">
        <button type="button" class="btn" id="clear-btn">Clear</button>
      </div>
      <form id="command-form" class="row">
        <input
          id="command-input"
          type="text"
          autocomplete="off"
          spellcheck="false"
          placeholder="id; uname -a; getprop ro.build.version.release"
          class="flex-1 text-[1.2rem]"
          disabled
        />
        <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary" id="send-btn" disabled>Send</button>
      </form>
      <p class="text-body text-[1.15rem]">
        Commands run as the daemon's uid on the device. Output streams back
        live; each command line is echoed above. Nothing is logged by this
        page beyond usage analytics.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <script>
    import '../scripts/analytics.ts'
    import '../scripts/terminal.ts'
    import '../scripts/support.ts'
  </script>
</Base>
